{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795526994,"nonce":"Y7V3AjY0PCx2VMZVkMPspdkns9A=","message":"Fewer passes per slide frame, d9b3db9154eb556ab2d9c963b022d67043564f7b (local, not pushed).\n\nWhy the opacity-group atlas skipped the calendar: each fading month sits alone inside a full-opacity wrapper layer (a named id nothing samples), so no two fading layers were consecutive in one node list, and the months' primitives carry a type-4 shape clip, which the atlas refused because the frame sits in the aux rows in batch coordinates. The atlas now looks through a wrapper that draws as its content, and gives each cell its own copy of a shape clip frame moved with the cell (frame length by kind as the shader reads it: 5 rows for type 4, 3 for the others, to the last point for a polygon).\n\nWindow, scripted swipe every 0.23 s (BLINC_BENCH_WHEEL_PERIOD=14), M1 Pro 2x:\n- Metal trace: slide frames 12 -\u003e 8 encoders, each its own MTLCommandBuffer; mean 12.66 -\u003e 8.47 per frame. The two Tight Blit passes and one layer pass per frame became one atlas draw and one Opacity Layer Pass.\n- Whole process, 80 swipes, /usr/bin/time -l interleaved: about 25.5e9 instructions (26.26, 25.82, 24.44) -\u003e about 22.3e9 (22.36, 22.04, 22.55), 12.5% less; user+sys 5.7 s -\u003e 4.6 s mean. One run of the new binary stalled before the swipes started (window idle, 4 s CPU) and was killed and excluded.\n- CPU over the swipe phase (ps): 30.0% before; 24.7% and 22.4% after.\n\nChecks: probe at COSTS_SCALE=2, 2 turns with every frame saved: 26 old/new frame pairs identical (0 pixels differ), cached equals full, and a temporary counter showed 14 two-cell atlases in the run, 6 with the shared shape clip. blinc_recorder headless --run-ignored all 385 passed; blinc_gpu + blinc_app --run-ignored all 767 of 768 (the tbc BC4 canary, fails by design); BLINC_NODE_TABLE=1 calendar and animation_paths 61 passed, app group tests 8 passed. New unit test of the moved frame; the atlas pixel test now has two wrapped groups sharing a rounded-rect shape clip and fails (pixels differ by 178) when the frame is not moved.\n\nWhat is left per slide frame: 8 command buffers (about 4 transit buffers wgpu inserts before each pass, the atlas draw and composite, one more layer primitives pass and the overlay pass). Structural frames and hit testing as 916db13.","files":null}]}